ssion,thefirstprob-\nlemthattheyusuallyencounteristhatofmulticollinearity.Manyofthemconclude\nthat there is something wrong with OLS; some resort to new and often creative\ntechniques to get around the problem. But, we tell them, this is wrong. Multi-\ncollinearityisGod’swill,notaproblemwithOLSorstatisticaltechniqueingeneral.\nWhat Blanchard is saying is that multicollinearity is essentially a data\ndeficiency problem (micronumerosity, again) and some times we have no\nchoice over the data we have available for empirical analysis.\nAlso, it is not that all the coefficients in a regression model are statisti-\ncally insignificant. Moreover, even if we cannot estimate one or more re-\ngression coefficients with greater precision, a linear combination of them\n(i.e., estimable function) can be estimated relatively efficiently. As we saw in\n28Blanchard, O. J., Comment, Journal of Business and Economic Statistics, vol. 5, 1967,\npp.449–451. The quote is reproduced from Peter Kennedy, A Guide to Econometrics,4thed.,\nMIT Press, Cambridge, Mass., 1998, p. 190.\n\nGujarati: Basic II. Relaxing the 10. Multicollinearity: What © The McGraw−Hill\nEconometrics, Fourth Assumptions of the Happens if the Regressors Companies, 2004\nEdition Classical Model are Correlated?\n364 PARTTWO: RELAXING THE ASSUMPTIONS OF THE CLASSICALMODEL\n(10.2.3), we can estimate αuniquely, even if we cannot estimate its two com-\nponents given there individually. Sometimes this is the best we can do with\na given set of data.29\nRule-of-Thumb Procedures\nOne can try the following rules of thumb to address the problem of multi-\ncollinearity, the success depending on the severity of the collinearity\nproblem.\n1. A priori information.Suppose we consider the model\nY =β +β X +β X +u\ni 1 2 2i 3 3i i\nwhere Y=consumption, X =income, and X =wealth. As noted before,\n2 3\nincome and wealth variables tend to be highly collinear. But suppose a pri-\nori we believe that β =0.10β ; that is, the rate of change o